        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 804,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 691,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order 1, I1, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the slope of I0, so it appears wherever I0 does and something is being fitted or differentiated - the mean direction of a von Mises distribution, for example, involves I1 / I0.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0 and antisymmetric (I1(-x) = -I1(x)): nearly a straight line x/2 near 0, then growing roughly like e to the |x|: I1(2) = 1.59, I1(4) = 9.76. It never oscillates.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity past about |x| = 90 in 32-bit floats;\nt.special.i1e avoids that.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I1(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4, drawn from -10 (bottom) to 10 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i1e is the version divided by e to the |x|. t.special.i0 is order 0. t.special.bessel_j1 is the oscillating cousin.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 675,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order 1, I1, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the slope of I0, so it appears wherever I0 does and something is being fitted or differentiated - the mean direction of a von Mises distribution, for example, involves I1 / I0.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0 and antisymmetric (I1(-x) = -I1(x)): nearly a straight line x/2 near 0, then growing roughly like e to the |x|: I1(2) = 1.59, I1(4) = 9.76. It never oscillates.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity past about |x| = 90 in 32-bit floats;\nt.special.i1e avoids that.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I1(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4, drawn from -10 (bottom) to 10 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i1e is the version divided by e to the |x|. t.special.i0 is order 0. t.special.bessel_j1 is the oscillating cousin."
        },
